{
	title: 'Persistent scheduled unattended jobs',
	spec: 'Hal provides /schedule list/add/pause/resume/remove in the shared transcript on both clients. Jobs persist timing text, prompt, creation cwd, client IANA timezone and model (default or aliases resolved each run, or an exact model). Creation uses ASON; required values are when and prompt. Missing timezone requires an explicit value rather than silently using the server zone. Listings show running/waiting/paused state, settings, next occurrence with timezone, and last/current clickable session/tab and result. Completion exposes operations, job IDs, setting names and model values. To change settings, remove and recreate the job. Every run opens an ordinary fresh unattended session that stays open, with normal tools and plugins including hal/tool. The cwd grants no trust; each run respects current project trust. Human interaction transfers control and restores normal notifications. Success is quiet; failures remain visible. Direct dependency-free timing supports durations, hourly minutes, weekdays, daily time lists and inclusive ranges with unambiguous ellipsis syntax. Clock schedules use real timestamps in the saved timezone: repeated fall-back minutes both occur, missing spring minutes skip. Jobs run independently without overlapping themselves; active occurrences skip. Downtime catches up once; uncertain runs after restart are stopped and recorded without automatic retry before general turn recovery. Persist only the current/last run reference and result; older output remains in ordinary session history. Validate stored IDs before opening sessions. V1 excludes continued-session runs, timeouts, automatic closing, guided forms, dedicated run/stop commands, and a separate past-run ledger. Target at most 250 formatted physical lines for command and scheduler together, with timing parsing separate; no helper relocation to disguise size. Tests cover timing/DST, fresh execution and model defaults, trust boundaries, overlap, restart catchup/non-replay, native tools and persisted controls.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Recovery initializes before general turn recovery. Saved run history boundaries distinguish a completed scheduled turn from older continued history; uncertain work is stopped and recorded, not replayed. Continued runs discard session trust overrides. Same-cwd trust revocation rebuilds the frozen prompt; cwd handoff keeps its existing prefix behavior. Native hal/tool execution uses ordinary prompts and turns.',
		'Verification: 63 focused timing, lifecycle, prompt-cache, context-handoff, autoclose, notification, trust, timezone and server tests pass; targeted lint passes. Full suite exposed unrelated convention failures and several integration timeouts; typecheck remains blocked by find-open event validation and plugin-sync-session test narrowing. Convention failures also reproduce with scheduler integration files at HEAD.',
		'Cost: timer scans jobs once per second; calendar lookup runs only when computing a next occurrence. Measured on the implementation server: ten weekly spring-DST lookups took 374.6 ms, one hundred took 3500.1 ms. All run records are retained; store serialization grows with history. No dependencies added. Host restart activates scheduler initialization.',
		'Supersedes the larger first implementation and its lifecycle/cost notes: v1 uses fresh sessions only and keeps one run reference/result per job. Removing continued history, timeout/autoclose settings, guided forms, edit/run/stop verbs and the duplicate historical ledger reduced the formatted command and scheduler to 76 + 174 = 250 physical lines (including imports, types and blanks); the independent timing module is 78 lines, down from 99 after removing its unused formatter. No helpers were moved elsewhere. Users change settings by removing and recreating a job, stop work in its ordinary session, and close accumulated tabs manually. The saved scheduler store grows with job count rather than total runs; normal session storage and open tabs still grow with runs. Timer work is linear in job count once per second. Recovery consumes no idle due occurrence before catchup; it records unfinished fresh runs without replay. This restores the no-overlap/no-uncertain-retry and current-trust invariants without a second full run history.',
		'Final small-v1 verification: 62 tests pass across schedule timing, scheduler lifecycle, prompt-cache, context-handoff, autoclose, notifications, project context, system prompts, clients and server. Changed five TypeScript files pass oxlint with zero warnings/errors; git diff --check passes. Typecheck reports only existing find-open event validation and plugin-sync-session test narrowing errors, not changed scheduler files. Host was not restarted. User review is still required for satisfaction with this v1 scope.',
		'SYSTEM.md requires scheduled jobs, cron entries and similar automation to use short, readable calls to existing functions, commands or scripts. Implementation belongs in named source files and reuses existing logic; job definitions are not embedded programs. User explicitly requested this guidance.',
		'Job lists show a bounded first-line prompt summary, timing, timezone, status and last run link/result; they do not duplicate full settings or programs. /schedule show ID exposes the full saved job, and completion includes show and IDs. This restores a readable list for real jobs in both clients.'
